# DESCRIPTION

The Vulkan layer is a driver in the Vulkan loader's registry, written on the core (gpucore(2)) and on nothing under it.
It is not a Vulkan driver for one GPU: a GPU gets Vulkan by implementing the core, and the layer makes the rest.
The loader reads its commands by name from `coreGetInstanceProcAddr` and `coreGetDeviceProcAddr`, in plain pointer types.
`vkCreateInstance` fails with `VK_ERROR_INCOMPATIBLE_DRIVER` where `CoreDriver.device` is nil or throws, so the loader goes on to its other drivers.
There is one physical device for the core device, one queue family that draws, computes and copies, and one queue.
It reports Vulkan 1.3 with buffer device address, timeline semaphores, dynamic rendering, synchronization 2 and multiview, and two device extensions: `VK_EXT_descriptor_buffer` (below), and `VK_EXT_custom_border_color` where the core's device has a sampler's own border colour.
Every feature, limit and format property it reports is the core device's: `VkPhysicalDeviceFeatures` and the 1.1, 1.2 and 1.3 feature structures are `DeviceFeatures` (a feature the device lacks is reported absent, never emulated), the limits are `DeviceLimits` (image sizes, array layers, sample counts, anisotropy, line widths, colour attachments, patch size, subgroup size, multiview views, the timestamp period), and `vkGetPhysicalDeviceFormatProperties`, `vkGetPhysicalDeviceImageFormatProperties` and their "2" forms are `DeviceLimits.formats(_:)`.
A device that is asked to enable a feature it lacks fails.
The heap's slots for the sampled images, samplers, storage images and the two kinds of texel buffer are the device's; four descriptor sets, 128 bytes of push constants and eight vertex buffers are the root block's (below).
What Vulkan has that the core has no word for is absent: sparse resources, geometry stages' adjacency topologies, more than one viewport, 16-bit and 8-bit storage, protected memory and presentation.
Dispatchable objects keep their first word for the loader, which puts its dispatch table there.

## Memory, buffers and images

There are three memory types, one for each intent of the core: 0, device-local (`.device`); 1, host-visible and coherent (`.upload`); and 2, host-visible, coherent and cached (`.readback`).
Memory of types 1 and 2 can be mapped; flushing and invalidating do nothing, since the core makes the bytes coherent when work is submitted and when the timeline reaches it.
A buffer is its memory and an offset, and its device address is the allocation's address plus that offset.
An image is a core image made by `vkCreateImage`: 1D, 2D, 3D, or a cube (an image made cube-compatible), of any mip levels, layers and samples the device offers, in any of the core's formats (every colour, packed, depth, stencil and compressed format it names).
Its memory requirements are nominal (every texel of every level, layer and sample, in any type) and binding memory to it records nothing, since the core places the image itself.
Layouts and layout transitions are ignored, since the core tracks them.
A view is a part of an image (levels and layers) in a format the image's may be viewed as (an image made with the mutable-format flag), as a 1D, 1D array, 2D, 2D array, cube, cube array or 3D view, with a component mapping (`VkComponentMapping`: each component the texel's own, 0, 1 or another of its components) which a sampled view keeps and a storage view of the same image does not (Vulkan reads a storage image as it is); a view of a depth and stencil image has one aspect to be sampled and both to be drawn into.
A 1D image is sampled, stored to and copied but never drawn into (it is `VK_ERROR_UNKNOWN`, as the core's rules say, to ask for a colour attachment of one).
An image used as an input attachment is also a texture of the core, so its views take texture slots like those of a sampled image.
A view of an image that is sampled takes a texture slot of the heap when it is made (unless it has both aspects), one of an image that is a storage image takes a storage slot (unless it covers more than one level), and each gives it back when it is destroyed.
A sampler takes a sampler slot when it is made, with all the state Vulkan gives it: the filters and the mipmap mode, an address mode for each axis, the border colour (the six standard ones, or `VK_EXT_custom_border_color`'s: a colour of the sampler's own, float or integer, read where an axis clamps to the border), the level-of-detail bias and range, anisotropy, comparison and unnormalised coordinates.
A buffer can be a texel buffer: `vkCreateBufferView` makes a core `BufferView` of the buffer's memory (the buffer's place plus the view's offset, the range or the rest of the buffer, the format), and takes a slot in the heap's array of uniform texel buffers if the buffer was made with `UNIFORM_TEXEL_BUFFER`, and in the array of storage texel buffers if it was made with `STORAGE_TEXEL_BUFFER`, giving each back when the view is destroyed.
A heap slot belongs to its view or sampler; writing a descriptor only writes the slot's number.
Where the device has no clears, a transfer destination that can be drawn into is also a render target, since `vkCmdClearColorImage` is then a pass that clears.

## Descriptors and pipelines

A set is a record in its pool's `.upload` memory, and the record is read when the work runs.
Binding `b`'s element `i` is at the layout's offset for `b`, plus 16 * `i`.
Every descriptor is 16 bytes:

- a uniform or storage buffer: the address (with its offset) and the range, 8 bytes each;
- a sampled image, a sampler, a storage image, an input attachment, a uniform texel buffer or a storage texel buffer: its heap slot, in 4 bytes (the input attachment's is the texture slot of its view, and a texel buffer's the slot of its buffer view in its own array);
- a combined image sampler: the texture's slot and the sampler's, 4 bytes each.

A write past the end of a binding's array goes on into the next binding.
`vkGetDescriptorEXT` writes the descriptor of an input attachment as of a sampled image; a texel buffer named by address, which has no view, is not provided.
A dynamic uniform or storage buffer has the same record as a plain one: the descriptor holds the buffer's address and range as written, and the dynamic offset is added by the shader, from the draw's root block (below).
A pipeline layout may have up to 16 dynamic descriptors, counting each element of an array, and `vkCmdBindDescriptorSets` takes exactly as many offsets as the sets it binds have dynamic descriptors.
A binding made with immutable samplers has their heap slots written into every set allocated from the layout, and a descriptor update leaves them alone: a write to such a sampler binding does nothing and a write to such a combined image sampler sets the image only.
A pool frees sets only when it was made to; a freed set's place is the next set's, and resetting the pool gives back all of them.
A pipeline layout has up to four sets and 128 bytes of push constants.

A shader module keeps its words.
A pipeline is made by running each stage through the lowering (vulkanlowering(2)), against the layout's sets and push constants and, for the vertex stage, against the vertex input state, and giving the result to the core.
Each stage's `VkSpecializationInfo` goes through the lowering with it, as the values of the constants by their ids (a map entry is 1 to 8 bytes, little-endian), so the core's pipelines have the constants as plain constants, the workgroup size of a compute shader included.
`CoreDriver.lowering` is that lowering.
A graphics pipeline has a vertex and a fragment stage, and a compute pipeline a compute stage; the entry point is named by the stage.
The vertex input state is a binding and a stride (a multiple of 4) for each of up to eight vertex buffers, and an attribute at a location with an offset that is a multiple of 4, in one of the formats `R32`, `R32G32`, `R32G32B32` or `R32G32B32A32` as `SFLOAT`, `UINT` or `SINT`, or `R8G8B8A8_UNORM`.
The topology is a triangle list or strip, a line list or a point list.
A pipeline that states its viewport and scissor has them sent before its draws.
The rest of the fixed state is the core's, each stated field for field: the depth and stencil state (where the pass has such an attachment), culling, front face, polygon mode, depth bias, depth clamp, line width, rasterizer discard, the samples, the sample mask, sample shading, alpha to coverage and alpha to one, the blend of each colour attachment (every factor and operation, and the colour write mask), the logic op, the blend constants, and primitive restart.
The topologies are the lists, strips and fans of points, lines and triangles, and patches with the tessellation stages; a geometry stage and the two tessellation stages are lowered as the stages they are.
The state a pass's commands set is the dynamic state: the viewport and scissor, the blend constants, the stencil reference, compare and write masks, the depth bias, the depth bounds and the line width.
A device without general blends has opaque and source alpha over for the colour, and a source alpha over for which the alpha's source factor is source alpha is drawn as one.
The targets come from the render pass's subpass or from `VkPipelineRenderingCreateInfo`: the colour formats, the depth and stencil format, and the view mask.
A pipeline cache keeps nothing, and its data is empty.

A render pass is run as the core's passes, one for each subpass, over the framebuffer's views: the colour attachments, their resolve attachments, the depth and stencil attachment and its resolve, and the subpass's view mask.
The first subpass to use an attachment gives it the render pass's load operation and its clear value (an integer attachment's clear is made by a clear of attachments after the pass begins), a later one loads what the one before stored, and the last gives it the render pass's store operation; the dependencies between subpasses are the core's own tracking.
A subpass's input attachments are textures: the attachment's view is a texture of the heap, and the shader reads it by a fetch at its own fragment's position (vulkanlowering(2)), so it reads what an earlier subpass drew there.
What makes that so is that each subpass is a pass of the core, which ends before the next begins, and a barrier of the core (the fragment stage's writes visible to the fragment stage's reads and writes) follows each; an attachment a later subpass reads is stored by the pass that last draws it, whatever the render pass's store operation says of the end of the whole pass, which then applies to the pass that last uses it at all.
`vkCmdBeginRendering` is the core's pass with its attachments, depth and stencil, resolves, layers, view mask and render area.
A render area short of the target is the core pass's own area.

## Commands

A command buffer records a `GPUCommandList`.
A draw or a dispatch writes a root block into the command buffer's arena, which is 64 KB chunks of `.upload` memory, and names its address as the core's root pointer.
The root block is 288 bytes:

- offset 0: the four bound sets' record addresses, 8 bytes each, 0 where none is bound;
- offset 32: the eight bound vertex buffers' addresses, each with its offset, 8 bytes each;
- offset 96: the 128 bytes of push constants;
- offset 224: sixteen 4-byte dynamic offsets, the bound sets' in the pipeline layout's order (the sets in order, each set's by binding and element), 0 where there is none.

The arena belongs to its command buffer until the timeline passes the value of the last submission that used it: a command buffer that is freed, reset or begun again, or whose pool is destroyed, gives the arena to the device, which frees it then.
The state commands set the dynamic state; the layer keeps it and sends the core what the pipeline in use leaves to commands, before the draw that needs it, where it differs from what the core has been told in the pass.
`vkCmdCopyImage`, `vkCmdBlitImage` and `vkCmdResolveImage` (and their 1.3 forms) are the core's copies, blits and resolves with the regions as given (mip levels, layers, offsets, the aspects, a flipped blit), and `vkCmdCopyBufferToImage` and `vkCmdCopyImageToBuffer` are its region copies, with the row length and image height, where the device has them; without them they copy the whole image, rows packed.
`vkCmdClearColorImage` and `vkCmdClearDepthStencilImage` are the core's clears of the ranges named (a colour by its format's channels: floats, unsigned or signed integers), `vkCmdClearAttachments` clears the rectangles and layers of the pass's attachments, and `vkCmdFillBuffer` and `vkCmdUpdateBuffer` are the core's, where the device has them; without them they write their bytes into the arena and copy them from there, and a colour clear is a pass that clears the whole image.
The counted indirect draws, `vkCmdDrawIndirectCount` and `vkCmdDrawIndexedIndirectCount`, are the core's.
A secondary command buffer is a list of its own, which `vkCmdExecuteCommands` appends to the primary's at that point (a secondary buffer that continues a render pass records inside the pass its primary has); its arena stays until the primary's work is done.
Events: an event is set when the submission that sets it is made, and waiting for events is the barrier of their stages.
Barriers keep their stages (the core's stages are compute, vertex, fragment and transfer, and a mask that names the ends of the pipe, the host or everything is all of them), and an image barrier also names its image and its levels and layers; inside a pass they do nothing.
A dispatch of no workgroups does nothing.
The timestamp, occlusion and pipeline statistics queries are the core's, where the device has them; a statistics query has one value for each counter of its pool, in the order of the counters' bits.

The commands return no errors, so what the layer can't record is kept and said at `vkEndCommandBuffer`.
Then the list is judged by `GPUCommandList.validate` against the device's limits and the capabilities of its formats, and what the device lacks (an indexed or indirect draw, an indirect dispatch, a query, a pass that loads its attachment, more than one draw in a pass, a copy, blit or clear, a state command) fails the end with `VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT`; `CoreDriver.message` says which.
A secondary buffer is judged with the primary that executes it.
Everything the layer leaves out fails the same way, where it is recorded, or at creation where it is made.
A shader the lowering can't take, or that isn't SPIR-V, fails the pipeline with `VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT` or the invalid-shader result, and the message says what the program used.

## Descriptor buffers

`VK_EXT_descriptor_buffer` is on the device.
A set layout's record is its descriptor buffer's layout: `vkGetDescriptorSetLayoutSizeEXT` is the record's size (to a multiple of 16) and `vkGetDescriptorSetLayoutBindingOffsetEXT` is a binding's offset in it.
`vkGetDescriptorEXT` writes the 16-byte descriptor of a uniform or storage buffer, a sampled or storage image, a sampler or a combined image sampler, in the format above.
`vkCmdBindDescriptorBuffersEXT` keeps up to four buffer addresses, and `vkCmdSetDescriptorBufferOffsetsEXT` makes the address plus the offset the bound set's record, as binding a set does; the shaders read it the same way.
The descriptor size of every type the device has is 16 and the offset alignment is 16; the device reports `descriptorBuffer`, with capture and replay, push descriptors and the other layouts' extras off.

## Synchronisation

The device has one timeline, and every fence and semaphore is a value on it.
Each submission signals the next value; it waits for the greatest value among its waits.
A fence is the value of the submission that signalled it, and a fence made signalled is reached already.
A binary semaphore is the value of the submission that signalled it, and a wait consumes it.
A timeline semaphore keeps the device value each of its values was signalled at: a wait for one of them waits for the first submission that signals it or a later value, a value the host signalled or the semaphore began with is reached at once, and `vkSignalSemaphore` by the host is reached at once too.
`vkQueueSubmit` and `vkQueueSubmit2` are the same; a fence given with no submissions is signalled by an empty one.
`vkWaitForFences`, `vkWaitSemaphores` and the idle waits wait for the device to reach the values; a timeout of 0 only looks.
The layer owns the device's timeline: it counts from the value the device has reached, and nothing else may signal on it.

## What it leaves out

What the device lacks, it refuses: the core's rules judge every image, view, sampler, pipeline and list, and an error is `VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT` for a feature or format the device doesn't have and `VK_ERROR_UNKNOWN` for a shape no device takes.
What Vulkan has and the core has no word for, the layer leaves out:

- Sparse images and buffers, and aliased image memory; the pass dependencies that name an input attachment (the layer ends a subpass's pass and begins the next with a barrier between them, whatever the dependencies say), and a multisampled or depth input attachment.
- Adjacency topologies, dynamic state past the core's (the 1.3 and extension states), and more than one viewport or scissor.
- Texel buffers by address (a descriptor buffer's `pUniformTexelBuffer` and `pStorageTexelBuffer`), inline uniform blocks, push descriptors, descriptor update templates, `vkCmdCopyQueryPoolResults`, and, with descriptor buffers, push descriptors, capture and replay, embedded immutable samplers and dynamic buffers (an immutable sampler's slot is not in a record the application writes).
- Float64, float atomics, depth bounds and tessellation on a device without them, and anything else the device's features say it lacks.
- Presentation (WSI), instance extensions, device extensions but `VK_EXT_descriptor_buffer` and, where the core's device has a sampler's own border colour, `VK_EXT_custom_border_color`, and queues but the one.
- Waiting for a semaphore value before any submission signals it.

